# Command surface (GUI wallet)

`tauri::generate_handler![...]` in `src-tauri/src/lib.rs` is the wallet's
entire command surface: what a page can invoke. It is held to the wallet
contract (`shekyl-core/docs/api/wallet_rpc.yaml`, `x-shekyl-method-registry`)
by `scripts/ci/check_command_surface.sh`, in three legs:

1. **Name.** Every registered command is a contract adapter — its name is a
   `SPECIFIED` method — or has a row in `scripts/ci/command_surface.conf`
   saying what it is instead: `SHELL <reason>` (app-shell concern with no
   contract analogue), `RENAME <method>` (the same operation under a GUI-only
   name; the drift ledger), or `COMPOSITE <methods>` (one round trip over
   several contract methods). A `REJECTED` name is never registered.
2. **Consumer.** Every registered command is invoked by a page, and every
   invoked name is registered. Tests are not consumers.
3. **Stub.** No registered command's body is an unconditional refusal. An
   absent feature is absent from the UI, not a registered "not available".

Feature-gated registrations (`#[cfg(feature = "...")]`) are outside the
default build: reported, not judged, except that a `REJECTED` name is refused
everywhere.

## Working with it

- **Adding a command:** name it as the contract does. If the contract has no
  such method, either it is app shell (add a `SHELL` row with the reason) or
  the contract is missing a method — which is a shekyl-core contract change,
  reviewed as one, not a GUI-only name.
- **Renaming toward the contract:** delete the `RENAME` row in the same PR;
  a row for a name that no longer exists fails the gate as stale.
- **Deleting a command:** delete its callers and its row in the same PR.
- **The gate goes red on a tree you did not touch:** the contract moved.
  Read `wallet_rpc.yaml`'s registry diff; a method newly `REJECTED` must lose
  its GUI command, not gain an allowlist row.
- **Never widen the gate to pass.** `scripts/ci/test_check_command_surface.sh`
  proves each leg can still go red; it runs before the gate in CI.

Sibling gates: shekyl-core `check_wallet_rpc_liveness.sh` (the RPC edge) and
this repo's `check_file_size_ratchet.sh` (rule 27).
